# Recovers from a terminated computation and says why it terminated.
# Resolves `value` to its normal form; if that resolution terminates, behaves
# as `alternative` applied to the message the termination carried, otherwise
# as `value`. The message goes by position, the way an atom hands one to a
# fallback, so `caught (2.as-i64.div 0.as-i64) I` answers "i64 cannot be
# divided by zero". `recovered` drops that message and this one keeps it,
# which is what lets a test say which failure it expects rather than only
# that there was one. An alternative with no void to take the message aborts,
# instead of losing it again. A termination that something forced on the way,
# such as a step of a `seq`, arrives with the location that `PhSafe` puts in
# front of the reason, since that is what the program prints at the top, so a
# test of such a failure asks whether the message ends with the reason.
#
# @todo #9145:30min Stop skipping 'mandatory-package' here. The lint keeps
#  the names of the objects that live directly under 'eo-runtime/src/main/eo'
#  in a list of its own, in 'org/eolang/funcs/home-object.xsl', and 'caught'
#  is not on it yet, so the lint asks this file for a '+package' it must not
#  have. Remove this directive once objectionary/lints#1539 adds the name.

+architect yegor256@gmail.com
+home https://github.com/objectionary/eo
+rt jvm org.eolang:eo-runtime:0.0.0
+rt node eo2js-runtime:0.0.0
+spdx SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) 2016-2026 Objectionary.com
+spdx SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+unlint mandatory-package
+version 0.0.0

[] > caught /A
  ? > value /A?
  ? > alternative /A

  eq. ++> can-hand-the-message-of-a-termination-to-the-alternative
    caught
      T
        "the reason it stopped"
      I
    "the reason it stopped"

  ends-with. ++> can-hand-the-message-when-a-step-of-a-sequence-terminates
    caught
      seq *
        T
          "the step that stopped"
        true
      I
    "the step that stopped"

  eq. ++> can-keep-the-value-when-not-terminated
    caught
      42
      I
    42

  eq. ++> can-tell-two-failures-of-one-object-apart
    caught
      div.
        i64 80-00-00-00-00-00-00-00
        -1.as-i64
      I
    "the smallest i64 divided by minus one is past the largest i64"

  caught --> stops-on-an-alternative-that-cannot-take-the-message
    T
      "the value that stopped"
    42

  caught --> stops-on-an-alternative-that-terminates-too
    T
      "the value that stopped"
    T
